\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces An illustration of the elements used to describe a feature. The feature is shown as the black dotted line. \emph {a} is the angle made by the tangent at the start point and the \emph {x}-axis; \emph {b} is the angle between \emph {a} and the line from the origin to the midpoint of the feature; $c_x$ and $c_y$ are the adjusted coordinates of the end point.\relax }}{38}{figure.caption.63}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Demonstration of the eight possible directions used for segmentation. The black lines deliminate the boundaries of each direction with the red numbers indicating the assigned values in each condition.\relax }}{42}{figure.caption.68}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Illustration of the technique used to combine similar sequential features. The touch points are shown in black. The red dots illustrate the starting points of the two respective features. \emph {a} and \emph {b} are the angles between each feature (red line) and the positive \emph {x}-axis. If the difference between \emph {a} and \emph {b} is less than 20 degrees, they will be combined into one feature.\relax }}{42}{figure.caption.69}
